Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-28-2001||08:07 PM||||Hi, <P>Anyone know what size the brake lines are on the 84 fiero SC?<P>Wanting to stop by the autoparts place on the way home and get the parts I need.<P>The main lines are 3/16th correct?   what size are the other ones?<BR>(at Canadian tire they come in 9ft. lenths)<P>also to replace ALL the steel lines to all wheels from the master cylinder, What size and how many fittings will I need?<P>Sorry to ask so many questions, just wanting to pick up everything at once if possible since I'm also renting the ISO flange tool & a tubing bender tonight after work and don't have my car here.. It's at home with a broken, rusted out brake line.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P> Thanks!!<P><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||06-28-2001||08:41 PM||||I am not sure but I think fieros are double bubble flare (unique to GM) not double flare.<P>CHECK TO SEE!!<P><BR>does any one know which. bubble or flare??||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-28-2001||10:26 PM||||I remember reading before in my haynes book that they were ISO double flare.. But I will double check when I get the flaring tool. Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>anyone know what size pipes/fittings?<P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||06-29-2001||12:16 AM||||Yeh the size is that one i can never remember.  I always have to ask too. <P> "I need some brake line, you know the normal size"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Brian||06-29-2001||06:56 AM||||As I remember it all the lines are metric.<BR>If you try to fit 3/16 line to the metric fittings it's a no go.<P>CTC does carry premade lengths with installed fittings of the correct type (bubble flair). These are fine for all but the main line running to the back & the line that crosses from the rear "T" to the passenger rear wheel.<BR>In both case the lines CTC (& others) are too short.<BR>I simply saved the old fittings or aquired new ones as required, took em to a brake shop & had them make up these two lines to length using the appropriate metric lines.<P>Hope that helps||||reg||1
Z||000005||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-29-2001||09:21 AM||||How many types of flares are there for brake lines?<P>Are there only 3? and GM are bubble/iso correct?<P>S.A.E. "double flare," ISO "bubble flare," hose-end flare, and JIC 37.5 degree single flare. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-29-2001||03:31 PM||||anyone?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000007||Jason Kamrud||06-29-2001||11:28 PM||||Last time I repaired brake lines it was at the right-rear where there's a tee between the back 2 brakes. What I used was just a 3/16 line with one flared end right off the shelf & a metric flare fitting. On the other end I just used a brass 'compression' union (normally used in plumbing) to join to where I had cut the broken piece off at.  It worked great, took under a half-hour to repair, and cost less than ten bucks.<BR>Just be sure to kind of hide the compression fitting in case your car ever gets inspected!<P>------------------<BR>Jason Kamrud||||reg||1
Z||000008||PBJ||06-30-2001||12:27 AM||||compression fittings are illegal for brake applications for a reason.||||reg||1
Z||000009||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-30-2001||07:57 AM||||Our vehicle inspections are every year.. <P>I'm renting the tools i need this weekend to do it properly so i never have to worry about brake failure.<P>I took my front trunk out last night...sure made it easier to reach everything  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>except for rusty stuck bleeders, replacing the brakelines are gonna be easy.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||PBJ||06-30-2001||11:40 AM||||start soaking those bleader screw with wd 40 or penatrating fluid of some type.  Best of Luck.<P>I tap on the very top of the bleader with a small hammer serveral times to aid in freeing<BR>them up.<p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 06-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-30-2001||12:21 PM||||Thanks, will try that also.. will heating them any help also? or will that ruin the seals? (even if I'm careful?)||||reg||1
Z||000012||PBJ||06-30-2001||12:25 PM||||I know some people recommend heat but I have never had one come loose with heat that didn't come loose by a soak and tapping with hammer.  The fiero calipers are also aluminum (as I'm sure you know) so that is going against you/us.   It is more luck than anything else.<p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 06-30-2001).]||||reg||1
